<!--Post: 11788--><!--Time: 1111175773-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11788"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Surfietx<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Surfietx<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 18, 2005 - 11:56 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I posted this in the &#34;buried in the backyard&#34; thread, but thought it worthy of discussion. <BR> <BR>Do you think perhaps that he was a cross dresser and therefore the reason for the confiscated pantyhose and jewelery in the shed/backyard?   <BR> <BR>Could it be that by killing other women he was actually symbolically trying to kill the woman in himself?  Of course, that doesn&#39;t explain the Otero murders, but it is still something to think about.  That too and the arranging of the shoes and clothing at the scenes? <BR> <BR>Maybe he dressed up in the victims&#39; clothing afterward?   <BR> <BR>Also, too, he tried to take on very masculine jobs.  In addition, he tried to be very active in church which is what many gay or cross dressing people do to be &#34;saved/cured&#34; from their affliction/disposition. <BR> <BR>I dunno...just something to think about. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
